CALIBRATION MODE, no graded answer -- declared outright in its own header at :29. The
deliverable is the fire-lane metric block (defFired, ttfShot min/mean/max, defShots, attShots,
casualties, cost-weighted losses) which lands in the verdict note and in debug.log per unit.
The script polls to resolution or deadline, formats that block, and exits. It compares nothing
against a bar.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS, and why this one is the sharpest case in the set. The header used to
justify its `Test.Pass` explicitly -- "never Test.Pass-less, that would be a demo" -- i.e. it
passed in order to avoid being classified as a demo and dropped by run-batch.sh's verdict-less
filter. That was a real constraint solved with the wrong verdict: `Test.Skip` satisfies the
same filter (the filter greps for Test.Pass|Fail|Skip), so the scenario still runs in --all,
while no longer contributing a green it did not earn. `Test.Skip` is the harness's own word
for "ran, produced no graded answer" (TestGlobal.cs) and run-batch.sh maps it to the SKIP
bucket.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever puts a bound on the metrics -- this scenario
was authored to produce the numbers a bound could be set FROM, so it is a genuine candidate
rather than a permanent diagnostic. A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades STALE/RED
on purpose: a scenario that STARTS grading shows up loudly instead of silently changing
meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail` declarations,
where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green. A timeout reaches
expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED -- which matters here,
because this scenario polls for a combat resolution and has a real deadline path.
